pouch boundary-line patch 0072 - the kernel owns the mask; sigsuspend, sigtimedwait, sa_mask.

Operator vote 24 (2026-11-03); ARCHITECTURE 8.8.3 and POUCH-DESIGN 6.4.

One mechanism restores a mask at a handler's return, in the kernel, for both phenotypes.
Once the kernel moves the mask at a handler's entry, at SYS_NOTED and across a
sigsuspend, a copy of it here is wrong in a handler and after one. So the copy goes.

  src/internal/_pouch_signal.h, src/signal/_pouch_signal.c, src/signal/block.c,
  src/thread/pthread_sigmask.c  __pouch_note_mask_shadow goes. SYS_NOTE_MASK swaps and hands
      back the old mask, so SIG_SETMASK is one call, and a read-modify-write is
      __pouch_note_mask_hold (block every note, return the old mask) and then the mask it
      wants. pthread_sigmask reads the caller's set before any swap, so a bad pointer faults
      with the mask as it was.
  src/signal/sigaction.c        The row carries SA_RESETHAND, SA_NODEFER and sa_mask in the
      flags word (bits 16..31).
  SIGPIPE (operator vote 25)    Its default is a row that ignores and no longer a mask bit.
      The restore at a handler's return undid a bit set in a handler, so signal(SIGPIPE,
      SIG_DFL) there left a default row and a clear bit, and the next write to a closed pipe
      terminated. A row also holds for every thread, which a bit never did. The constructor
      writes it, __pouch_pipe_mask goes, and the dispatcher writes it back after a one-shot,
      since the kernel resets to its own default.
  src/signal/_pouch_signal.c    The dispatcher takes x2, the row's flags, and under
      RESETHAND resets the table with a compare from the handler it read, so a peer's
      sigaction that has landed since stands. The name-to-signal table is
      __pouch_note_to_sig, shared with sigtimedwait. __pouch_sigset_to_take gives each tty
      signal its own row. An SA_SIGINFO handler gets a real siginfo_t, si_signo and SI_KERNEL
      with a zero union, where it used to find the dispatcher's x1 and x2. Its ucontext_t *
      is NULL.
  src/signal/sigsuspend.c       SYS_NOTE_WAIT's suspend: the mask and the sleep are one step.
  src/signal/sigtimedwait.c     SYS_NOTE_WAIT's take. Upstream's EINTR loop stays, because
      sigwait may not read EINTR, but a retry waits only for what is left of the timeout: a
      steady note would otherwise keep the timeout from passing. sigwait, sigwaitinfo and
      sigpause sit on these unchanged.
  src/select/poll.c             Operator vote 28. poll's body moves to the hidden __pouch_poll,
      which takes a mask, and __pouch_poll_wait is the one call: SYS_poll with no mask,
      SYS_NOTE_WAIT's poll form with one, so that the swap and the wait are one step.
  src/select/ppoll.c            The mask goes through, where it was ignored.
  src/select/select.c           The same split, as __pouch_select. A failed call went through
      __syscall_ret twice, and the second one read syscall_cp's -1 as the native error and
      turned it into EIO, so every failed select read EIO and an interrupted one never read
      EINTR. A closed fd in a set is EBADF for the whole call, with the sets as they were: it
      was a POLLNVAL no set counted, so select answered 0 at once, as a timeout does.
  src/select/pselect.c          The mask goes through, where it was ignored.
  src/signal/sigpending.c       SYS_NOTE_WAIT's pending form, and __pouch_take_to_sigset, so a
      tty signal shows as itself. It called the SYS_rt_sigpending sentinel, which read ENOSYS.
  src/signal/sigsetjmp_tail.c   Through pthread_sigmask. It called the SYS_rt_sigprocmask
      sentinel, so sigsetjmp saved nothing and siglongjmp restored nothing.
  arch/aarch64/bits/syscall.h.in  __NR_note_wait 141. After a musl re-vendor, extend 0001's
      awk filter: m["note_wait"]="141";

Witnesses: pouch-hello-signals `suspend-*`, `sigpause`, `sa-mask`, `nodefer`, `one-shot`,
`one-shot-row`, `handler-mask`, `setjmp-mask`, `pipe-*`, `siginfo`, `take-*`, `ppoll-*`,
`pselect-*`, `sigpending`, `select-eintr`, `select-badfd`.

